# evidence-for: US-006, FR-019, AC-023, AC-024
#
# FR-019, AC-023, AC-024. Three Flask routes whose verbs are declared past a
# paren that is not syntax: one inside a `#` comment, one inside a string
# literal, one carrying neither. The join stopped when the parentheses balanced
# over the raw text, so the first two stopped before `methods=`, and what
# reached the verb decision was a text with no methods= in it -- which is also
# what a route declaring none looks like. The GET default fired and published a
# verb those endpoints answer 405 to. cfafe8e published nothing for any of the
# three, so GET on /exports was a regression rather than a miss.
--- cfafe8e: no decorator joining at all, so nothing is published ---
--- 339a80d: a paren inside a comment or a string balances the call early ---
GET /exports src/api.py:6
GET /imports src/api.py:15
GET /health src/api.py:24
--- HEAD: depth is counted over the parens that are code (AC-023, AC-024) ---
POST /exports src/api.py:6
PUT /imports src/api.py:15
GET /health src/api.py:24
